CASES=[
dict(id='X-T15-014',case='recovery',title='Statement synchronization with staged semantic results and diagnostic retention',objectives=['T15.14.a','T15.14.c','T15.13.c'],refs=[0],
problem='Implement tc_recover by composing the actual Pd lexer, Pratt syntax tree and checked interpreter. Parse semicolon-terminated expression batches, discard only failed statements and retain absolute diagnostic spans. The concrete 1+;22;3*4; batch must yield22 and12 with one error. A syntactically valid overflowing statement must produce a semantic diagnostic instead of a fake value. Implement the named newline-synchronization transfer without changing the retained parser.',
necessity='A compiler that stops at the first error cannot collect later independent diagnostics; a parser that resumes one byte too late loses valid input. Syntax success is distinct from safe evaluation. Recovery strategies discard context deliberately and can cascade after an inserted delimiter; binary-frame resynchronization does not establish this language contract.',
reasoning='Prove every delimiter is consumed exactly once and the next statement begins at its successor. Explain the staged ownership of token/tree/result storage, and why no failed statement publishes a value. Derive separate512-byte scan admission,256-byte imported segment limits,16-result/16-diagnostic capacities and whole-batch failure atomicity. The budget counts scan positions, not every imported instruction. Give the counterexample to treating newline synchronization as a semantics-preserving optimization.',
verification='Use an independent list-of-statements/value table on valid, malformed, empty, overflowing and missing-terminator cases. Check actual absolute offsets, survivors and diagnostic statuses; force17 successes,17 errors,257-byte segment and every fuel boundary. Kill the resume-one-byte-late and semantic-error-suppression mutants for different reasons. Run both delimiter policies on the same bytes and record changed acceptance.',
criteria=['Retain correct absolute source spans while consuming each synchronization delimiter once; diagnose the first damaged statement without corrupting the next.','Compose actual syntax and checked evaluation with separate diagnostics; failed statements cannot fabricate values and batch capacity/fuel failures preserve caller state.','Bound scan, parser storage and recovery work separately; transfer to newline synchronization and falsify overstated context preservation.'],
hints=['Write the half-open segment [begin,delimiter) before parsing it.','Keep token/tree/value temporaries private until that statement succeeds.','Use 1+;22; to distinguish skipping the delimiter from skipping its successor.'],
variant='Switch from semicolon-only statements to semicolon-or-newline synchronization. Before execution predict 1\\n2;, an unterminated final expression, an unmatched parenthesis followed by a delimiter and an inserted delimiter inside a would-be expression. Implement the policy, retain prior semicolon cases and explain why quoted strings or multiline grammar would need a different lexer boundary.',
analysis='The safe publication unit is one successful statement inside a staged whole batch. An input 1+;22; exposes the skip-successor mutant by changing22 into2. A valid parse of2147483647+1 exposes the separate semantic failure. All original Pd errors remain diagnostics; missing final delimiter is105. Budget length+1 is admitted before parsing, then counted in the scan; storage overflow can reject the entire staged result. Recovery guesses must be described as such: (1+;2; intentionally discards the first incomplete context. Newline mode is a language change, not a universal repair. Require a fresh witness and explanation of retained/dropped state, not merely the expected survivors.'),
dict(id='X-T15-015',case='layout',title='Document algebra, continuation-sensitive grouping and AST-preserving formatting',objectives=['T15.14.c','T15.13.c','T15.15.c'],refs=[1],
problem='Implement typed NIL/TEXT/LINE/CAT/NEST/GROUP documents and the specified bounded layout interpreter. Include the pending continuation when choosing flat groups. Implement tc_expression_doc over an actual successful PdTree and reparse every width to the same syntactic tree, even when evaluation overflows. At width4, group(nest(2,a LINE b)) CAT zzz must emit a newline and two spaces; width6 can flatten it.',
necessity='Concatenating preformatted strings loses grouping and indentation semantics. A group that fits alone may overflow when followed by unbreakable text. Equal evaluated values do not prove that formatting preserves syntax, and an acyclic shared DAG can unfold to far more work than its unique node count.',
reasoning='Derive the CAT unit/associativity, flattening and nesting laws, and the explicit pending-frame invariant. Explain the precise greedy probe rule and why it is not a claim of global optimality or a lazy language implementation. Prove failure atomicity and termination under node/fuel/output bounds, including cumulative nesting and repeated DAG references. For expression formatting preserve operator kinds and child structure, not source offsets or merely values.',
verification='Compare thousands of widths, text lengths, indentation and continuations with an independent layout formula; supplement with nested groups and actual parse-format-parse tree equality. Force a cyclic/forward child, oversized indentation, low fuel and output exhaustion. Kill ignore-continuation and lose-nesting mutants separately. Retain a simple reference and explain both unbreakable overflow and repeated-lookahead resource costs.',
criteria=['Separate syntax from presentation and interpretation through an actual PdTree-to-document adapter; preserve structural parse round trips across width changes.','Implement explicit document stacks, continuation-sensitive fit decisions and resource refusal with no caller-output mutation; diagnose unique-node versus unfolded-work confusion.','Preserve an independently structured layout oracle and transfer to a trailing unbreakable continuation, nested groups and shared DAGs without overstating optimality.'],
hints=['A group decision sees both its child and the remaining work stack.','NEST changes subsequent line indentation, not the leading text column.','Fully parenthesize the adapted tree first; optimize parentheses only under a separately proved rule.'],
variant='Add an unbreakable suffix after an already constructed group, then vary width below/at/above the combined flat length. Revisit nested groups under a flat outer group and a DAG that reuses a child twice under a small fuel budget. Predict output or unchanged-output refusal and preserve actual syntax-tree round trips. Explain which Wadler laws carry over and which optimality/complexity claims do not.',
analysis='The counterexample group(a LINE b) followed by zzz separates isolated-group fit from continuation-sensitive fit. NEST has no effect before the first break. The C algorithm uses a copied probe stack and counts probes in the same fuel budget; it is an explicit deterministic greedy specification, not the source lazy implementation. Children with smaller indices prevent structural cycles, but repeated references can expand exponentially, so unique-node size alone is not a total-work bound. The formatter uses complete parentheses and compares kinds/children/values/root after reparsing, allowing changed source locations. Reject constant folding as a solution:1+2147483647 has valid syntax although checked interpretation fails.'),
dict(id='X-T15-016',case='template',title='Restricted template bytecode, scoped list application and contextual escaping',objectives=['T15.14.c','T15.13.c','T15.15.c'],refs=[2,3],
problem='Compile the shared literal/field/presence/list/static-include grammar into typed operations, then interpret it over a readonly four-field model. Implement nested list item shadowing/restoration, include-cycle detection and whole-output staging. Compare plain-text and HTML-text modes using synthetic <tag>&. Preserve actual model bytes and ensure braces in model values are data, never new directives.',
necessity='Template syntax, model computation and output-context encoding are different mechanisms. A readonly-looking interface does not automatically enforce formal model-view separation. Repeating a first list item can pass singleton tests; recursive includes or nested expansion can exhaust resources without a pointer defect. Escaping appropriate for an HTML text node is not a universal sanitizer.',
reasoning='Derive paired block jumps, lexical item scope, active-include identity and byte/fuel/output bounds. State which separation properties the instruction set enforces and which require stable model construction discipline. Explain one-pass escaping and why already-escaped data is encoded again rather than treated as trusted syntax. Distinguish an observable local markup-shaped output violation from demonstrated script execution.',
verification='Compare compiled C evaluation against an independent immutable template-tree interpreter for absent/present-empty fields, multiple values, nested loops, includes, escaped braces and invalid directives. Force executed versus unexecuted cycles, missing includes, depth/fuel/output failure and model immutability. Reject the original no-escaping mutant on a local harmless string and the repeat-first-item mutant on a two-element list.',
criteria=['Keep compiled template syntax, model values and rendered output distinct; implement paired blocks, list scope and static includes without callbacks or model writes.','Bound malformed input and expansion, stage output on errors and demonstrate the exact local encoding defect/repair with its restricted HTML-text scope.','Maintain an independent tree interpreter and transfer nested list/include composition between plain-text and HTML-text contexts, including cycles and unchanged-output failure.'],
hints=['Compile block starts and matching ends before evaluation; reject an unmatched stack.','Pass the current item as scoped interpreter state; a nested loop must restore the outer item afterward.','Map each original character once; do not feed replacement entities back into the encoder.'],
variant='Use a nested list body that invokes a static include while retaining/restoring the outer current item. Change the output contract from plain text to HTML text, including quotes, ampersands, literal directive-shaped model values and an already encoded ampersand. Add a conditional include cycle that is absent on one model and executed on another; force fuel/output refusal and explain why attribute/script contexts remain outside this encoder.',
analysis='The compiler rejects computation and dynamic include names; the interpreter has no method-call or mutation operations. These restrictions enforce only part of Parr separation: another C alias can mutate a model, and model constructors can still smuggle layout-shaped data. The active-template set detects only executed include cycles, correctly leaving an absent branch unevaluated. Loop item pointers have call scope and are restored on return. HTML-text encoding maps original characters once, so &amp; becomes &amp;amp;; suppressing that because input resembles an entity would trust untrusted syntax. The flawed teaching mutant only demonstrates a string property violation. No network/browser or script execution is needed or claimed. The independent model is an immutable syntax tree, not the C jump instruction sequence.')]
